---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import SourcesStandards from '../../components/SourcesStandards.astro';
import CalculatorDisclaimer from '../../components/CalculatorDisclaimer.astro';
import { calculatorWebPage } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why does pump pressure scale with the square of stroke rate?',
    a: 'Circulating pressure losses are friction losses, and friction pressure loss through a fixed system rises with the square of flow rate. Double the strokes per minute and pressure doesn\'t just double — it roughly quadruples, which is why the new-strokes formula squares the ratio rather than using it directly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does new pressure loss scale directly with mud weight, not squared?',
    a: 'Friction pressure loss is also directly proportional to fluid density — a heavier mud is simply harder to push through the same pipe and annulus at the same rate. Unlike the flow-rate relationship, that\'s a straight-line (linear) relationship, so the new-mud formula uses a direct ratio rather than a squared one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a slow circulating rate (SCR) and why does this matter for it?',
    a: 'An SCR is a pump pressure measured and logged at a reduced, pre-agreed stroke rate (commonly a couple of rates such as 30 and 45 SPM) so a kill-rate pressure is already known before a well control event, rather than guessed at during one. This calculator\'s squared stroke-rate relationship is exactly how a driller checks or scales an SCR reading to a different rate without re-pumping at every possible speed.',
  },
];

const webAppLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment#app",
  "name": "New Pump Pressure Calculator: Stroke Rate & Mud Weight Changes",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment",
  "description": "Free calculator for new circulating pressure after a stroke-rate change or a mud weight change, using the standard IWCF proportionality formulas.",
  "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
  "applicationSubCategory": "Drilling engineering calculator",
  "operatingSystem": "Any (web browser)",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isAccessibleForFree": true,
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" }
};

const webPageLd = calculatorWebPage(webAppLd, { lang: 'en' });

---
<BaseLayout
  title="New Pump Pressure Calculator: Stroke Rate & Mud Weight"
  description="Free calculator for new circulating pressure after a stroke-rate change or a mud weight change, using the standard IWCF proportionality formulas."
  extraJsonLd={[webAppLd, webPageLd]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }, { label: 'Pump Pressure Adjustment', href: '/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Calculator</div>
    <h1>New Pump Pressure <span>Adjustment</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Work out the new circulating pressure after a stroke-rate change or a mud weight change, without re-measuring at surface.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="calc-box">
          <div class="calc-title">🧭 New Pump Pressure With New Strokes</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ppa-p1">Current Pressure (psi)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ppa-p1" value="1500" step="1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ppa-spm1">Current SPM</label>
              <input type="number" id="ppa-spm1" value="80" step="1">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ppa-spm2">New SPM</label>
              <input type="number" id="ppa-spm2" value="100" step="1">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:16px;">
            <div class="output-line headline" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">New Pump Pressure</span><span class="output-value" id="out-newp">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-box mt-24">
          <div class="calc-title">🧭 New Pressure Loss With New Mud</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ppa-op">Original Pressure Loss (psi)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ppa-op" value="2222" step="1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ppa-mw1">Original Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ppa-mw1" value="9" step="0.1">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ppa-mw2">New Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ppa-mw2" value="9.1" step="0.1">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" style="margin-top:16px;">
            <div class="output-line headline" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">New Pressure Loss</span><span class="output-value" id="out-newpl">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <CalculatorDisclaimer lang="en" context="Confirm an actual SCR reading at the planned kill rate before relying on a scaled figure for a real well control event — this calculator checks the proportionality math, not a substitute for a fresh pressure measurement." />
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The Formulas Used</h2>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">New Pump Pressure With New Strokes</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>P2 = P1 × (SPM2 ÷ SPM1)²</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block mt-24">
          <dt class="formula-label">New Pressure Loss With New Mud</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>P2 = P1 × (Mud Weight2 ÷ Mud Weight1)</code></dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>Worked Example</h3>
        <p>An SCR of 1,200 psi was logged at 50 SPM. What's the expected pressure at the normal drilling rate of 75 SPM?</p>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">New Pump Pressure With New Strokes</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>P2 = 1200 × (75 ÷ 50)² = 1200 × 2.25 = 2,700 psi</code></dd>
        </dl>
        <p>Now say that same string later gets weighted up from 9.6 ppg to a 12.0 ppg kill mud, with the 2,700 psi figure above as the starting pressure loss:</p>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">New Pressure Loss With New Mud</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>P2 = 2700 × (12.0 ÷ 9.6) = 2700 × 1.25 = 3,375 psi</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">The new SCR to use on the kill sheet once the heavier mud is actually in the string and circulated to surface.</dd>
        </dl>

        <h3>Where This Fits</h3>
        <p>Both calculations answer the same practical question from different directions: what should the pump gauge read once something changes, without pumping at the new condition first to find out. The stroke-rate version is what sets the slow circulating rate (SCR) pressures used directly on the <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet">kill sheet calculator</a> — SCR readings are normally taken at reduced rate, then this same squared relationship is what a <a href="/careers/driller">driller</a> uses to sanity-check pressure at any other rate. The mud-weight version matters any time mud is weighted up or down mid-well and pump pressure at the same rate needs re-establishing before it can be compared against a baseline for a kick or washout indicator.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SourcesStandards lang="en" ids={['api13c']} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .output-line.headline .output-value { color: var(--amber); font-size: 18px; }
</style>

<script is:inline src="/scripts/calculators/pump-pressure-adjustment.js" defer></script>
